{
  "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
  "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Richards",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0000-0000",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In this case, inmate Kyle Richards alleges that Thomas Perttu, a prison employee, sexually harassed Richards and other inmates. Richards also alleges that, when he attempted to fle grievance documents about the abuse, Perttu destroyed them and \u201cretaliated against\u201d him for attempting to fle them."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0000-0007",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Magistrate Judge concluded that there was \u201ca genuine issue of fact as to whether Plaintiffs were excused from properly exhausting their claims due to interference by Perttu\u201d and that the issue was \u201cappropriate for resolution during an evidentiary hearing.\u201d"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0000-0008",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The usual practice is that factual disputes regarding legal claims go to the jury, even if that means a judge must let a jury decide questions he could ordinarily resolve on his own."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0000-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Prison Litigation Reform Act of 1995 (PLRA) requires prisoners with complaints about prison conditions to exhaust available grievance procedures before bringing suit in federal court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0001-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We begin with a settled premise: PLRA exhaustion is a standard affrmative defense."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0001-0001",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "that, in cases of intertwinement, district courts should struc-\nture their order of operations to preserve the jury trial right."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0001-0002",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Nothing in the PLRA prevents holding a jury trial here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0001-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Two Circuits have suggested they agree with Perttu that factual fndings related to exhaustion have no estoppel effect, but with little analysis and in cases that did not squarely present an estoppel issue."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0001-0004",
      "kind": "holding",
      "opinion_part": "majority",
      "pages": {
        "end": 18,
        "start": 18
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The principle of Beacon Theatres still applies when judicial resolution of a common issue might \u201cprevent a full jury trial\u201d for some reason other than estoppel."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0002-0000",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If Congress had expressly provided in the PLRA that ex-\nhaustion disputes must be resolved by judges, then we would\nhave been required to consider today whether such a provi-\nsion violates the Seventh Amendment. But it is a \u201ccardinal\nprinciple\u201d that we not address such a constitutional question\nunless necessary. Tull v. United States, 481 U. S. 412, 417,\nn. 3 (1987)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The judgment of the United States Court of Appeals for\nthe Sixth Circuit is affrmed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0002-0001",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Court does not get into this back-and-forth\u2014and here,\nI agree with the Court. We did not take this case to deter-\nmine whether the Seventh Amendment requires jury trials\nfor all disputes about exhaustion. There is no circuit split\non that question, and the court below did not address it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Richards",
      "case_id": "scotus-23-1324",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d9bcfe43d5d8593b765f8ba8dacca1517d1601e340f4c84c2eb9cfe519861c48",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d9bcfe43d5d8-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Richards argues only that he has a constitutional right to a\njury trial. Both his Brief in Opposition and his merits brief\nfocus exclusively on the Seventh Amendment."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
